# 02 · Design rationale

This page gives the hardware needs of the workload and the five platforms that the study compares.
Numbers from the checkpoint are **derived** ([01](01_workload.md)). Design constants are in
`configs/hw/*.yaml`, each with a source key or the word *assumption*. Results are **modeled**
([04](04_results.md)).

## What the workload asks for

1. **Almost every byte is a weight, and a token uses almost none of them.** The checkpoint is
   {{ gb(W.bytes.total_with_draft, 0) }}. One token touches {{ gb(W.touched_per_token.total, 1) }} of it:
   8 of 384 experts in each of 48 layers, and attention.
2. **A GPU must move those bytes in each step.** The weights are in HBM and the arithmetic is in the
   GPU. Thus the weights of each token go across the memory interface. With the draft model on, a step
   verifies 8 rows and reads about {{ gb(W.dspark_step.bytes_total, 0) }}. At 4x B300, that is a minimum
   of several milliseconds per step before any arithmetic.
3. **The arithmetic is small and narrow.** {{ pct(W.bytes.share_by_class_main.nvfp4, 0) }} of the
   weight bytes are NVFP4: a 4-bit code multiplied by a 4-bit code, with a scale per block of 16. On
   the GPU, arithmetic is {{ pct(g4.time_by_cause_batch1_ms.compute / g4.batch1.step_ms, 1) }} of a batch-1 step.
4. **A single request is launch-bound.** The serving engine issues about {{ n(NK.kernels_per_step, -1) }} dependent kernel launches
   per speculative step. We counted them in its source (tokenspeed-src). The fit gives each launch a
   time of {{ n(CG.params.kernel_overhead * 1e6, 1) }} µs.
5. **Long context is a separate problem.** Eight of the 48 layers attend over the full history. At the
   1,048,576-token limit, a step reads {{ gb(I.context.kv[-1].kv_full_bytes + I.context.kv[-1].kv_draft_bytes, 0) }}
   of KV cache. The location of the weights does not change this.

Thus there are three costs to decrease: launches (software), weight movement (memory architecture)
and KV movement (memory bandwidth). Each cost needs a different solution. The design space below
deals with one cost at a time.

## The platforms

| | what it is | weights are in | what it removes | status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **G0** | B300 GPUs that run the best published engines of today | HBM | nothing: the baseline | calibrated to published speeds |
| **G1** | the same GPUs with dataflow-style software: one compiled schedule, no dependent launches | HBM | launch waits | projected |
| **A** | a decode chip on HBM (MDA-1 from our DeepSeek-V4.1 study, re-targeted) | HBM | launch waits, GPU power and price | proposed |
| **S** | reloadable weights on the chip (MDS-1) | on-die SRAM, adjacent to the multipliers | weight movement | proposed |
| **F** | fixed weights (MDF-1) | mask-programmed storage | weight movement and reloadability | comparison point only |

**G0 gets its best published setup.** The setup is DSpark speculation on, MXFP8 KV cache, and TP4 with
expert parallelism. The lead baseline is the faster of two B300 layouts. We fit its four free
constants to nine published figures ([06](06_simulator.md)).

**G1 is the software counterfactual.** If software can do something on installed GPUs, we must not
credit it to silicon. G1 keeps the HBM efficiency and the attention kernels of the GPUs and removes
dependent launches. Its speed gain is {{ x(R["g1_x4/g0_x4"].speed_batch1) }} at batch 1.

**A tests whether a better datapath on the same memory is enough.** For speed it is not enough. At batch 1 it
is {{ x(R["a_x4/g1_x4"].speed_batch1, 2) }} of G1, because the two platforms wait on HBM. This was hypothesis H1, and the
results confirmed it. The value of A is power and price. With prefill counted, it is the cheapest
whole system in the study ({{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.a_x4.cost_gain) }} lower cost than GPUs).

**The study is about design S.** It is the speed design. Each weight is in SRAM adjacent to the arithmetic that uses it.
The weights of a layer are spread over a tile of dies. The hidden state of a token moves from tile to
tile, and the weights do not move.

The system loads the weights of S at boot. Thus the same hardware serves a new model version, a
fine-tune or a different model of the family. S is the correct comparison for a customer who retrains.

**F is the bound.** If the weights are fixed in the masks, storage becomes several times denser and
the datapath becomes simpler. F shows how much of the cost of S comes from reloadability. It is not a
proposal. A model with fixed weights cannot follow a retrain, and the card of this model already
describes several checkpoints.

