## Why these memory choices

| memory | bandwidth to the arithmetic | density | reloadable | role here |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| HBM | {{ n(G_HW.hbm_bw / 1e12, 1) }} TB/s per B300 (vendor figure) | highest | yes | weights on G0, G1, A; KV cache on all platforms |
| on-die SRAM | all banks read in parallel, in {{ n(I.designs.s.t_scan_us, 2) }} µs per pass | {{ n(1 / S_HW.area.mm2_per_mib_sram, 1) }} MiB/mm² with overheads | yes | weights on S |
| mask-programmed storage | as SRAM | {{ n(1 / F_HW.area.mm2_per_mib_rom, 1) }} MiB/mm² with arithmetic | no | weights on F |
| LPDDR | {{ n(I.kv_memory.lpddr_pool_bytes_per_s / 1e12, 2) }} TB/s for a 16-package pool | high, cheap | yes | KV option; S with it is slower than A above about {{ n(I.kv_memory.crossover_context_vs_a, -3) }} tokens |

The answer to the question "which memory architecture" is a split: **weights in on-die SRAM, KV cache in HBM.** The
weights are large, mostly idle and read in fixed patterns. Thus we can spread them across many dies and
hold them at retention voltage. The system writes the KV cache for each token, and eight layers read
it in bulk. Thus the KV cache needs one pooled, high-bandwidth memory adjacent to one shared attention engine.

We considered a hybrid (hot experts in SRAM, cold experts in HBM) and removed it because of time. The
routing sweep ([04](04_results.md), section 10) gives a limit for its possible gain. With the most
skewed routing measured on open models, the top 20% of experts get 80% of the picks.

## What decides the result

In order of effect on the headline ([04](04_results.md), section 12):

1. The time per weight operation of S. We took it from a published machine and did not assume it. It decides speed.
2. Token demand. It decides how many systems share the design cost, and thus the cost per token.
3. The speed that "interactive" needs. On cost, S is equal to GPUs at 100 tok/s per user and much better at 200.
4. The size of the shared attention tile. It limits the throughput of S, and it is cheap to make larger.
5. Power management of idle weight banks. It decides the batch-1 energy result.
